00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.230
This is Marie.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:50.791
Like almost everyone who lives in Burundi,
one of the poorest nations on earth, with

00:02:50.791 --> 00:02:58.120
a per capita GDP of about $800, she lives
in a small, rural village.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.900
Why is Marie poor?

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.870
There are a hundred different “macro”
answers — a lack of foreign investment,

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:11.540
corruption, red tape, the absence of property
rights, and so on.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.350
Eliminate these impediments, history suggests,
and economic development naturally progresses

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.740
through a series of discrete stages.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:24.290
A nation moves from agriculture to manufacturing
to services and consumption.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:30.490
It’s a known and predictable formula — the
kind of rapid transformation epitomized by

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:31.790
East Asia.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:38.680
It’s how you go from here… to here in
the span of a few decades.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:44.100
But before any of that can happen, there are
a few prerequisites — “starting conditions”,

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.460
if you will, that we tend to overlook.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.130
You might say that the real reason Marie is
poor is that she can’t sell her family’s

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:53.130
crops.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.200
She can’t sell her family’s crops because
there are no paved roads to get to and from

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.200
the nearest market.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.290
And there are no paved roads because the area
is infested with disease-carrying mosquitoes.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:10.540
These very basic problems like mosquitoes
ultimately prevent Burundi from even reaching,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.920
much less climbing, the economic development
ladder.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.650
It’s what you might call a “Poverty Trap”.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.889
You may have heard of it as something like
“without a car, you can’t get a job.

00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:24.879
And without a job, you can’t get a car”.

00:04:24.879 --> 00:04:27.860
In this case, it’s just much more extreme.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:32.090
So, how does a nation escape this trap?

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.919
How does one get to the first rung of the
ladder?

00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:41.469
Jeffrey Sachs, a famous economist and director
of Sustainable Development at Columbia, argued

00:04:41.469 --> 00:04:44.530
the solution was quite simple.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.469
Instead of tackling Malaria, then moving on
to clean drinking water, and so on — each

00:04:49.469 --> 00:04:54.759
of which he said was insufficient on its own
— all these things needed to be solved at

00:04:54.759 --> 00:04:55.819
once.

00:04:55.819 --> 00:04:58.430
It was called the “Big Push”.

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.240
To end extreme poverty, in other words, you
must address all the “root causes” at

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.389
the same time.

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:11.020
After you’ve killed all the mosquitoes,
cleaned all the water, and built all the roads,

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.590
everything else will follow.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:19.080
These steps are akin to “clearing the runway”,
eliminating obstructions, allowing the plane

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.139
to take-off on its own.

00:05:22.139 --> 00:05:27.059
So confident was he in this theory, that he
decided to launch an experiment, called “The

00:05:27.059 --> 00:05:30.789
Millenium Villages Project” in 2005.

00:05:30.789 --> 00:05:37.449
It would begin as 14 sites in Kenya, Uganda,
Tanzania, Mali, and elsewhere.

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:42.449
Once these were successful, he promised, they
would become the proof of concept for a giant,

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:47.540
global Marshall Plan to end extreme poverty
once and for all.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:53.940
Not only would it not be very hard, but, according
to Sachs, neither would it even be expensive.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:06:00.370
Ultimately, he said, this was an investment,
not an act of charity.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:05.610
These massive, life-changing upgrades to health,
agriculture, education, and infrastructure

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:10.029
would cost just $120 per villager.

00:06:10.029 --> 00:06:16.699
It was “teach a man to fish” in practice,
at scale, and without compromising on corruption.

00:06:16.699 --> 00:06:19.449
It all sounded pretty intuitive.

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:24.900
Instead of just parachuting-in free stuff,
they would teach skills, create jobs, and

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.169
encourage the creation of sustainable businesses.

00:06:28.169 --> 00:06:32.289
Who doesn’t want to believe that you can
permanently pull someone out of extreme poverty,

00:06:32.289 --> 00:06:36.750
and all for about a day’s wages in a rich
country?

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.990
It was a seductive idea.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:42.389
It also fit our preconceptions about poverty.

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:47.409
All these people needed was a little outside
help to get going on their own.

00:06:47.409 --> 00:06:54.279
Each Millenium Village was guided by a 147-page
handbook written by 29 experts, most from

00:06:54.279 --> 00:06:56.389
Columbia University.

00:06:56.389 --> 00:07:00.819
It was the summation of everything we know
about alleviating poverty — from specific

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:06.219
instructions to increase crop yields to goals
for community involvement.

00:07:06.219 --> 00:07:11.479
Schools were built, ambulances delivered,
clinics constructed, clean water provided,

00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:13.949
and practically everything in-between.

00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:20.939
Crucially, the project hired its own teams,
completely bypassing corrupt officials.

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:26.279
But it wasn’t long before the handbook,
written in New York, collided with reality,

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.979
in rural Africa.

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:34.129
Corn, for instance, was chosen as the main
crop in Uganda for sensible, scientific reasons

00:07:34.129 --> 00:07:39.499
— it’s resilient, nutritious, and not
very difficult to grow.

00:07:39.499 --> 00:07:44.419
Sure enough, the first crop was a major success
— with the average yield doubling in only

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:45.990
a year.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.430
In fact, there was far more than the village
could possibly eat.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:55.069
There was just one problem: there was nowhere
to put it.

00:07:55.069 --> 00:07:59.259
Not only were there no storage facilities,
but without any nearby markets, there was

00:07:59.259 --> 00:08:02.180
no way to sell it without losing money.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:08.240
To make matters worse, only after investing
$300,000 on the high-yield crop did the project

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:14.880
managers find out that Southern Ugandans don’t
actually like corn — they called it “prison-food”.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.589
Realizing this, Sachs scrambled to find a
buyer for the corn, eventually convincing

00:08:19.589 --> 00:08:23.960
the World Food Program to buy 150 tons of
kidney beans instead.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:30.020
This, they thought, was the key to success
— commercial agriculture, allowing the Ugandan

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.490
village to sell its crops on global markets.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:38.350
But to do so profitably, it was calculated,
the village would need to produce three crops

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:41.900
a year, which demanded much more water.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:47.150
Unfortunately, there was no nearby water in
this Southern Ugandan village.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:53.310
The closest source was over 3 miles away — which,
of course, they had to walk to get to — much

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:55.320
of it uphill.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.910
Sachs then managed to convince an American
company to donate $150,000 worth of pipes

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.410
to transport the water.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:07.210
But it was later discovered that just shipping
the pipes would cost another $120,000.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:14.740
“Scrap that”, they decided, and opted
to import eight donkeys to do the job of transporting

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:17.400
the water, instead.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:23.910
Despite all that work, however, half the donkeys
died of exhaustion after only a few months.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:29.160
Now, each of these mistakes may seem trivial
and preventable on its own.

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:34.060
The project could’ve simply asked Ugandans
which crops they preferred in advance, for

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.430
instance.

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:40.770
But taken as a whole, the theme is clear:
there can be no true ‘handbook’ to end

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:41.820
extreme poverty.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:47.650
There’s no single, universal formula, model,
or theory.

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:52.700
Time and time again, the project ran into
unexpected hurdles — many of which would’ve

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:57.150
been plain impossible to anticipate, nevermind
prevent.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:00.160
The villages had no way to deal with crime,
for example.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:04.400
Nor was there a chapter for natural disasters.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:09.730
Like any human, many of the villagers sometimes
acted in ways you might describe as “economically

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.030
irrational”.

00:10:11.030 --> 00:10:16.820
For example, spending less money on fertilizer
than the handbook explained was optimal.

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:21.600
The project’s managers were frustrated to
find villagers using mosquito nets on goats,

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.700
rather than children.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:28.880
So much of its success depended on trust,
for which there’s no quick or easy equation,

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.750
and in some cases had been burned through
early failures, like what happened to the

00:10:32.750 --> 00:10:34.560
donkeys.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.650
This is not to say the project was a total
defeat.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:43.430
Malaria decreased almost immediately and primary
school enrollment soared.

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:48.450
But when all was said and done, one independent
researcher calculated that it ended up costing

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.520
$12,000 per household lifted out of poverty.

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:57.380
For context, that’s about 34 times the average
local annual income.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:02.370
What’s more, we don’t even know whether
those gains lasted after the project ended

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:06.320
in 2015, or if they were just temporary.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:10.110
In fact, it’s hard to say how many of the
project’s successes would’ve happened

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.910
anyway.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:16.250
Without a control group, the best we have
are estimates — one of which concludes that

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:21.250
only half the purported results were actually
due to the project.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:26.010
Some research goes a step further, suggesting
aid often does more harm than good because

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:28.800
of unintended consequences.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:32.670
Professor Nathan Nunn writes, quote, “We
may have our largest…

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:33.700
effects on alleviating…

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:38.870
poverty if we focused on restraining ourselves
from… harming less-developed countries rather

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:41.110
than… fixing them.”

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.991
Tariffs and anti-dumping duties suggest that
aid is often based more on the needs of the

00:11:45.991 --> 00:11:48.990
giver than those of the receiver.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:54.450
Now, “foreign aid” is a broad concept
and some types, like disaster relief, are

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.000
unquestionably positive.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:03.610
The point is not that foreign aid can’t
work, but that right now it largely doesn’t.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:08.720
Consider how powerful that $12,000 that it
cost to lift one household out of poverty

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:14.420
would be if simply given, no strings attached,
to that family.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:19.110
Even if you just invested that money, giving
the interest to the family directly, that

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:25.070
would be, at a 5% interest rate, $600 a year
forever.

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:29.470
The unequal effects of climate change will
drastically increase the need for foreign

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:31.110
aid this century.

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:37.660
So, until and unless we discover that seemingly-mythical
‘formula’ to end extreme poverty, it’s

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:44.910
worth asking: What if we just gave that $3.5
trillion in foreign aid directly to the people

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:45.930
who need it?

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:51.930
At the very least, rich countries could stop
imposing onerous conditions on aid — something

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.750
China has often been held-up as a model example
of.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:59.540
It offers money to developing countries with
no strings attached.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:04.480
Yet, for all its efforts, China has few, if
any, friends.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:11.000
If its objective is to “buy the world’s
love”, it doesn’t seem to be succeeding.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:16.170
There may be an overlooked, historical reason
why China has such a hard time making allies

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:21.130
— a fundamental difference in the way it
sees itself and others.

